From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sr-0w <Sr-0w@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 12:55:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] media: i2c: scan one camera slot at a time

Powering the three Sony slots together resets the handset before anything
reaches the log, while the Samsung slot scans cleanly. Taking them one at a time
is what tells them apart, so name the slot by its I2C bus number rather than
turning them all on with a single boolean.

Signed-off-by: Sr-0w <Sr-0w@users.noreply.github.com>
---
 drivers/media/i2c/hotdog-cam-scan.c | 16 +++++++++++-----
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/media/i2c/hotdog-cam-scan.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/hotdog-cam-scan.c
@@ -31,9 +31,10 @@
  * fault while scanning left the handset without a usable userspace. Load with
  * scan=1 by hand, with the log in view.
  */
-static bool scan;
-module_param(scan, bool, 0444);
-MODULE_PARM_DESC(scan, "Power each camera slot and probe its bus");
+static int scan = -1;
+module_param(scan, int, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(scan,
+		 "I2C bus number of the one slot to power and probe, -1 for none");
 
 /* One slot at a time: the two masters of a CCI block share an interrupt. */
 static DEFINE_MUTEX(hotdog_cam_scan_lock);
@@ -115,9 +116,16 @@
 	unsigned int i;
 	int ret;
 
-	if (!scan)
+	/*
+	 * One slot at a time, named explicitly. Powering all of them together
+	 * resets the handset, and doing them individually is what tells the
+	 * slots apart.
+	 */
+	if (scan < 0 || scan != i2c_adapter_id(client->adapter))
 		return -ENODEV;
 
+	dev_info(dev, "scanning this slot on i2c-%d\n", scan);
+
 	for (i = 0; i < HOTDOG_CAM_SCAN_NUM_SUPPLIES; i++)
 		supplies[i].supply = hotdog_cam_scan_supplies[i];
 
--
